 general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & ={c}_{1}{\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2{c}_{1}-{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & =-2[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]-4[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-2[\\begin{aligned}5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534211 17592186045417 true] [17592186414138 1068 #edb/ref 17592186414134 13194139534211 17592186045417 true] [17592186414138 1069 "q-333911" 13194139534211 17592186045417 true] [17592186414138 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534211 17592186045417 true] [17592186414138 1071 "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 3 \\\\ -6 & 3\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above.\nFind the unknown function $f(t)$ in the general solution to the system, shown below.\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}f(t) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n*Hint: The matrix has the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=3\\text{i}$ with the corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1-\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$*" 13194139534211 17592186045417 true] [17592186414138 1072 #uuid "ee5bc47e-f5eb-5ac3-a599-35150942e1b0" 13194139534211 17592186045417 true] [17592186414138 1074 "We need to find the eigenvalues and associated eigenvectors of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 3 \\\\ -6 & 3\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nWe are given the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=3\\text{i}$ and the corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1-\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Note:** The eigenvector corresponding to the second eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{1}$ is \n${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}.$ But we do not require this eigenvector for solving our system.\n\nThe solution that we obtain for the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=3\\text{i}$ and its associated eigenvector is\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}1-\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3\\text{i}t}.\n$$\n\nWe now express this as a real function. Using Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}1-\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3\\text{i}t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1-\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)-\\text{i}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+2\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t))+\\text{i}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+2\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}]+\\text{i}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+\\text{i}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${w}_{1}$ and ${w}_{2}$ are linearly independent. Therefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & ={c}_{1}{\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $f(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).$" 13194139534211 17592186045417 true] [17592186414139 1068 #edb/ref 17592186414131 13194139534211 17592186045417 true] [17592186414139 1069 "q-334217" 13194139534211 17592186045417 true] [17592186414139 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534211 17592186045417 true] [17592186414139 1071 "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{aligned}]x(t),\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n*Hint: One of the eigenvalues of the matrix is ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2+3\\text{i}.$*" 13194139534211 17592186045417 true] [17592186414139 1072 #uuid "ff1d5823-2f0a-574c-b322-55ba80787b34" 13194139534211 17592186045417 true] [17592186414139 1074 "We need to find the eigenvalues and associated eigenvectors of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nWe are given that the eigenvalue is ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2+3\\text{i}.$ \n\nLet's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors. For ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2+3\\text{i},$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{1}I=[\\begin{aligned}-3\\text{i} & 3 \\\\ -3 & -3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & \\text{i} \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Note:** The eigenvector corresponding to the second eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{1}$ is \n${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}.$ But we do not require this eigenvector for solving our system.\n\nThe solution that we obtain for the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2+3\\text{i}$ and its associated eigenvector is\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{(-2+3\\text{i})t}.\n$$\n\nWe now express this as a real function. Using Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{(-2+3\\text{i})t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+\\text{i}(-{e}^{-2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)) \\\\ {e}^{-2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\text{i}({e}^{-2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t))\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}+\\text{i}[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t} \\\\  & ={\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+\\text{i}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${w}_{1}$ and ${w}_{2}$ are linearly independent. Therefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & ={c}_{1}{\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}+[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534211 17592186045417 true]] :edb/tempids {"answer-05327d3a-aca0-5fb0-9c50-b10761df4f82" 17592186414105 "answer-1995fd1a-c6d4-512a-a6be-f138f303bc89" 17592186414106 "answer-1eac8192-0924-5878-a2a1-c2642849f722" 17592186414107 "answer-230a6570-5874-5514-a5c8-4c7623269609" 17592186414108 "answer-2de80169-b4ac-56d0-adbd-4e3603b33509" 17592186414109 "answer-2f3224fb-ee3c-5228-98d8-9c46e3a55abf" 17592186414110 "answer-38bc55e9-86ef-5ac7-9ae6-d59e96ad20ef" 17592186414111 "answer-39b3b68f-c430-5202-8335-2fa76a32426e" 17592186414112 "answer-3ddc94ff-178d-54f3-8514-a6004bb21fed" 17592186414113 "answer-53c71df4-1354-58a1-acaf-bec387c53d6e" 17592186414114 "answer-88a4b756-a1e7-55d5-ae31-17af65e53575" 17592186414115 "answer-97c43b65-4471-50b5-be1a-5209d4680a94" 17592186414116 "answer-ab461043-8644-5af4-a2d9-4fdfd11ef66e" 17592186414117 "answer-b06b432f-2968-54b1-befd-eb3d08349e0c" 17592186414118 "answer-b573e05d-11ff-53a0-89be-3bf5e5e75031" 17592186414119 "answer-be296864-0cbf-5b2e-b34f-c30c23610631" 17592186414120 "answer-be3193d0-4469-592a-86ed-cb959d386c16" 17592186414121 "answer-c04d436b-9385-5183-b55c-ddd19d0fabb8" 17592186414122 "answer-d25673bc-3c0e-530d-842d-2f585720adfa" 17592186414123 "answer-d5da709f-f968-5b54-a45f-00c909f7d831" 17592186414124 "answer-dace2